---
id: 38339d0e-d27c-4609-9205-82af3d2b585f
title: Anaphylaxis
---

# Anaphylaxis

- **Definition** - a potentially life-threatening, acute, severe, systemic allergic reaction
- **Clinical features of anaphylaxis** - acute, immediate onset of Sx from allergen exposure: 
![](../img/clinical_features_of_anaphylaxis.png)
  - <u>Skin and mucosal manifestations</u> - e.g. urticaria, conjunctival injection, angioedema of the limps or mucous membrane
  - <u>Respiratory manifestations</u> - respiratory comprimise due to upper airway (e.g. angioedema of the tongue), or laryngeal obstruction (e.g. stridor, wheezing, SOB, hypoxaemia)
  - <u>Cardiovascular manifestations</u> - hypotension and a/w end-organ dysfunction (e.g. syncope, incontinence)
  - <u>Gastrointestinal manifestations</u> - crampy abdominal pain, diarrhoea, repetitive vomiting
- **Salient points of Hx:**
  - <u>Possible allergens identified in association w/ onset of Sx</u> - onset of Sx within minutes or hours of intake of food, contact w/ latex, insect venoms or drugs: 
  ![](../img/common_allergens_causing_anaphylaxis.png)
  - <u>Potentiating factors</u> - exercise or alcohol can lower the threshold for an anaphylactic event
  - <u>Detailed allergic Hx</u> - detailed Hx of the past 24h is helpful if allergen not immediately obvious
- **Signs of anaphylaxis:**
  - **Primary assessment** - airway, breathing and circulation, disability and exposure:
    - <u>Airway</u> - angioedema of the limps, tongue, mucous membrane (airway potentially comrpimised)
    - <u>Breathing</u> - check SpO2 for hypoxaemia:
      - Inspiratory stridor suggestive of laryngeal oedema
      - Generalised wheeze suggestive of bronchospasm
    - <u>Circulation</u> - generalised oedematous state, distributive shock, reflex
    - <u>Disability</u> - LOC due to syncope may occur
    - <u>Exposure</u> - generalised urticaria
- **DDx of anaphylaxis** - anaphylactic mimics:
  - <u>DDx for LOC</u> - cardiac arrhythmias, MI, vasovagal syncope, hypoglycaemia
  - <u>DDx for respiratory distress</u> - status asthmaticus
  - <u>DDx for laryngeal obstruction</u> - foreign body, C1 inhibitor deficiency, idiopathic angioedema
  - <u>DDx of generalised flushing</u> - systemic mastocytosis, carcinoid syndrome
  - <u>Others</u> - e.g. phaeochromocytoma
- **Anaphylactoid reactions** - non-specific degranuation of mast cells by drugs, chemicals or other triggers and does not involve IgE:
  - <u>Drugs</u> - ASA, NSAID, radiocontrast media, opiates
  - <u>Physical</u> - exercie, cold
  - <u>Idiopathic</u> - no cause identified in up to 20% patients
- **Ix:**
  - **Serum mass cell tryptase concentration** - useful for retrospective Dx:
    - <u>Timing</u> - taken at 0h and \> 6h
    - <u>Interpretation</u>:
      - Rise of \> 20% from baseline (i.e. \> 6h) is diagnostic
  - **Specific IgE test or skin prick test** - to identify the allergen subsequently
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx** - anaphylaxis is an acute medical emergency:
    - <u>General measures</u> - prevent further contact w/ allergen, secure airway, breathing and circulation
    - <u>Prompt intramuscular adrenaline</u> - administer IM adrenaline promptly and repeat if initial response indequate
    - <u>Additional pharmacological therapy</u> - IM or IV antihistamines +/- IV corticosteroids
    - <u>Patient education</u> - education patient and family on how to prevent subsequent episodes, and how to react

    
    ![](../img/emergency_mx_of_anaphylaxis.png)
  - **General measures** - primary assessment and Mx:
    - **Airway** - consider <u>endotracheal intubation</u> if LOC and/or evidence of laryngeal oedema
    - **Breathing:**
      - <u>Give O2</u> to maintain SpO2
      - <u>Nebulised beta-agonists</u> to decrease bronchoconstriction
    - **Circulation** - IV access; IV fluid (20 ml/kg) to restore or maintain BP
  - **IM adrenaline** - delayed administration a/w increased mortality:
    - <u>Dosing</u> - 0.5 mL 1:1000 solution for adults
    - <u>Repeated dosing</u> - repeat at 5-min intervals if initial response inadequate
  - **Additional pharmacological therapy:**
    - <u>IM or IV antihistamine</u> - chlorphenamine
    - <u>IV glucocorticoids</u> - hydrocortisone 200 mg IV
  - **Patient education:**
    - Strict allergen avoidance
    - Anti-histamine for milder reactions
    - Adrenaline autoinjector (AAI) and emergency medical attention

    
    ![](../img/prescription_of_adrenaline_autoinjector.png)
- **Poor prognostic factors** - increasing risk of death with:
  - Poorly-controlled pre-existing asthma
  - Delayed treatment with adrenaline
